<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>Comparisons of core-set algorithm with a cold start versus a warm start. We repeat five separate runs for cold start and report the mean and standard deviation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The cold start problem in core-set algorithm refers to the challenges that arise from the random initialization of seed data. This random selection can introduce significant variability in the performance of the algorithm, as the quality of the resulting core-set heavily depends on the seed data points. In some cases, random initialization may lead to poor representations of the underlying data distribution. To improve the robustness and stability of the traditional core-set algorithm, we adjust the initialization process by selecting the sample with the lowest average similarity to others, thus enhancing the diversity of the core-set distribution. To verify the effect of the warm start, we conduct comparative experiments on the three datasets based on whether the core-set method randomly selects seed data. The experimental results are displayed in Table <a href="#A4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ D.1 Improvement on Core-set Algorithm ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental Results ‣ ALLabel: Three-stage Active Learning for LLM-based Entity Recognition using Demonstration Retrieval" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, which illustrate that our improved core-set algorithm with a warm start can stably achieve performance close to or even exceeding the upper bound of the traditional core-set algorithm.</p>
</div>
</section>
